<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    WPR.Engine.GameLoop - the game-loop subsystem: the contract for driving a game's run loop
    (IGameHost, GameHostState, TeardownPhase) AND the launch sequence that drives it
    (ApplicationLaunch), plus the per-title lifecycle table (GameLifecycleQuirks).

    ApplicationLaunch moved here from WPR.Backend.FNA on 2026-09-20. It was in the FNA backend
    because it was moved there VERBATIM from WPR.Runtime in Stage 4, when it still named
    FNAPlatform and FNA's spine types directly. Both of those went away underneath it: the spine
    is WPR.Framework.Xna's since 2026-09-01, and the windowing calls go through the
    WPR.Xna.Rhi.IPlatformBackend seam. What was left naming FNA was four lines - the title
    location, the leaked-window check, and two diagnostics - and those are now IGameLaunchHooks,
    implemented by FnaGameHost. Everything else in the file is host coordination: the collectible
    ALC and its Resolving handlers, the WP7 lifecycle priming, the per-game trace log, and the
    ordered teardown. None of that is a property of the rendering backend, so none of it belongs
    in one. The same rule that put composition in the engine tier ("a contract belongs with the
    subsystem that composes it") puts the launch sequence beside the contract it drives.

    THE TEARDOWN ORDER INSIDE ApplicationLaunch.Start IS LOAD-BEARING. It was moved byte-for-byte
    apart from the hook substitutions; see the remarks in the method before reordering anything.

    Dependency notes:
      * WPR.Framework.Xna / .Phone / .Silverlight - the launch sequence resets their per-launch
        singletons on teardown and primes the WP7 lifecycle. Engine -> framework is the
        established direction (WPR.Engine and WPR.Engine.Audio already do it); the frameworks
        never reference back.
      * WPR.Database (the Application row) and WPR.Loader (AssemblyNameStandardization,
        HardcodedAchievementCatalogue) are why the TFM list mirrors WPR.Backend.FNA rather than
        the plain net8.0;net10.0-android of the other engine projects: on Windows WPR.Database has
        no plain net8.0 leg, so a bare net8.0 here would fail restore with NU1201.
      * WPR.Engine.Input - the keyboard-driven tilt/touch emulation components are attached to
        the freshly created Game from here, exactly where the backend used to do it.
      * WPR.Engine.Sensors / .Vibration / .Audio - teardown resets the accelerometer subscribers,
        stops the motor, and the per-game log records which audio module served the run.

    THIS PROJECT DISSOLVED WPR.Abstractions (2026-09-01). That project was meant to be the
    linchpin every layer implemented; it ended up holding 14 types of which 11 had no consumer at
    all, and the three that did belonged next to the registry that hands them out - IAudioTranscoder
    to WPR.Engine.Audio, IAccelerometerProvider to WPR.Engine.Sensors, and IGameHost here. The
    lesson worth keeping: a contract belongs with the subsystem that composes it, not in a shared
    bucket named after the fact that it is abstract.

    A backend referencing this still does NOT pull in the platform-composition root (WPR.Engine),
    which is what keeps a backend free of the composition root's opinions.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(OS)' != 'Windows_NT' ">
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0;net10.0-android</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup Condition=" '$(OS)' == 'Windows_NT' ">
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
    <TargetFrameworks>net8.0-windows10.0.17763.0;net10.0-android</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <CheckEolWorkloads>false</CheckEolWorkloads>
    <AssemblyName>WPR.Engine.GameLoop</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>WPR.Engine.GameLoop</RootNamespace>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <SupportedOSPlatformVersion Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' == 'net10.0-android'">24.0</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Common\WPR.Common.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Database\WPR.Database.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Loader\WPR.Loader.csproj" />
    <!-- SilverlightAppHost, for the mixed-mode path only: MixedModeGame boots a Silverlight
         Application over the XNA loop and reuses that boot rather than carrying a second copy.
         One-way — WPR.Runtime does not reference this project (the game-loop host moved OUT of
         it in Stage 4), so there is no cycle. -->
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Runtime\WPR.Runtime.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Framework.Xna\WPR.Framework.Xna.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Framework.Phone\WPR.Framework.Phone.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Core\WPR.Framework.Silverlight\WPR.Framework.Silverlight.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Engine\WPR.Engine.Input\WPR.Engine.Input.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Engine\WPR.Engine.Sensors\WPR.Engine.Sensors.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Engine\WPR.Engine.Vibration\WPR.Engine.Vibration.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="$(SolutionDir)Engine\WPR.Engine.Audio\WPR.Engine.Audio.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
